  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\u27e8\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1\u27e9$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\u27e8\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,-1\u27e9.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ while the second equation gives $y=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$",
